=======================================================================
 test_poisson_sampler - targeted tests of the Poisson disk sampler
=======================================================================

WHAT THIS IS

  Six small decks that each aim at one fault found in the C++ Poisson
  disk samplers (poi_2D_*.cpp, poi_3D_*.cpp), in 2D and 3D, three
  seeds each: 18 point sets, judged against the EXACT h field.

  These tests FAIL on the original sampler (16 of 18 rows) and PASS
  with the fix, applied Oct 11 2026.
  Part of run_all.sh since the fix went in.

QUICK START

  run_poisson_sampler_tests.sh                  about 40 seconds
                                                 (LaGriT ~15 s, mostly
                                                 samp_V3; the rest is
                                                 the checker)

  then open logs/summary.html.

  Original sampler: 16 of 18 rows FAIL (U2 seeds 1 and 2 pass).
  Fixed sampler: all 18 PASS (macOS, Oct 11; V3 seed 1 has 2 pairs at
  0.994 h, within the 0.95 h allowance).

  Requires: LaGriT, Python 3 (standard library only), a POSIX shell.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
THE TESTS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  test  deck          field                          box
  U2    samp_U2.lgi   uniform h 0.02                 unit square
  U3    samp_U3.lgi   uniform h 0.05                 unit cube
  L2    samp_L2.lgi   h = 0.01 + 0.04 y              unit square
  L3    samp_L3.lgi   h = 0.03 + 0.12 y              unit cube
  V2    samp_V2.lgi   fine band at y = 9 (see below)  0.205 x 10 strip
  V3    samp_V3.lgi   fine band at y = 9              1 x 10 x 1 box

  V: h = min(hmax, max(hmin, hmin + 0.5 |y - 9|)), hmin 0.01 / hmax
  0.05 (2D), 0.03 / 0.15 (3D): a band about 0.2 wide where h is small,
  in a box 10 long, so the original lookup has drifted far by y = 9.

  What each catches:

  U  the neighbour search stops one cell short on the + side of each
     axis, so an accepted vertex there is never compared with a new
     candidate: interior pairs closer than h even with uniform h.
     (Weak in 2D: on some seeds no pair happens to fall there.)
  L  the same with variable h, where it also shows the lookup's
     off-by-one cell size.
  V  the h lookup: one cell size for every axis, the lower-corner
     node, no clamp. The fine band is read too far toward +y, so
     vertices pack too close just below y = 9 and the band above it
     is left with holes.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
RESULTS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  One row per output (e.g. V3_s2 = test V3, seed 2): PASS or FAIL.
  Exit status nonzero on any FAIL. Exact definitions are in the
  opening comment of check_poisson_sampler.py.

  HARD checks:
    every row  written, vertices > 0, all inside the box, no duplicates;
               LaGriT exit 0 and no POISSON DISK error block
    U, L       no interior pair closer than max(h_p, h_q)
               (exact: interpolating a uniform or linear field is exact)
    V          no interior pair closer than 0.95 max(h_p, h_q), and no
               gap wider than 2 h (probe points: distance to the
               nearest vertex over h there)

  Only INTERIOR pairs are judged: the sampler places box edge and face
  vertices with smaller radii (0.81 h, 0.9 h) on purpose.

  Sandbox results (the sampler compiled on its own; same logic as
  LaGriT, see PROPOSAL.md):

    original  16 of 18 FAIL (U2 seeds 1, 2 pass)
              V3: 8 200-8 500 pairs < 0.95 h, closest 0.30 h, gap 2.7-2.9 h
    fixed     18 of 18 PASS (V3 seed 1: 2 pairs at 0.994 h, allowed)

  Your real numbers will differ point by point (float sin/cos differ
  between macOS and Linux), not in the verdicts.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
READING THE PICTURE
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  One picture per row, projected on (x, y); 3D shows a slab around
  mid z. Grey dots: vertices (paler: on the boundary). Red lines:
  interior pairs closer than the limit. Orange +: gaps wider than
  1.5 h. V tests show the window y 8..10, with a dashed line where
  the fine band belongs (y = 9): on the original sampler the red
  band sits below the line and the orange holes just above it.

